Santiago:
<i> In walks Edwin Land.</i>

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:44.717 line:0%
He was kind of a chemist
more than a photographer.

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:47.586
Bonanos:<i> He was a Harvard
 student when he invented
 the polarizing filter.</i>

00:04:47.619 --> 00:04:51.757
<i> It's a filter that allows
 the amount of light getting
 through to be controlled.</i>

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:53.059 align:start size:56% position:44%
So that was
his first product,

00:04:53.092 --> 00:04:54.694
and in fact
that built the company.

00:04:54.727 --> 00:04:56.896
Man:<i> Cool-Ray Polaroid
 sunglasses.</i>

00:04:56.929 --> 00:04:58.798
<i> See about them.</i>

00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:00.633
Depew:<i> In the beginning
 of Land's pursuits,</i>

00:05:00.666 --> 00:05:04.070 line:0%
<i> he had no idea he would be
 in the camera business.</i>

00:05:04.103 --> 00:05:07.039 line:0%
<i> In 1943, he was on vacation
 with his family.</i>

00:05:07.072 --> 00:05:09.041 line:0%
<i> And he takes a picture of
 his three-year-old daughter,</i>

00:05:09.074 --> 00:05:12.478 line:0%
and she says,
"Dad, why can't I see
this picture right now?"

00:05:12.511 --> 00:05:14.447
Bonanos:
<i> The story goes that he spent
 the rest of the evening</i>

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.917
<i> walking around the resort
 and worked out the rough plan</i>

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:19.885
for how one would make
an instant camera.

00:05:19.918 --> 00:05:23.022
 Depew:<i> So it was in 1943 that
 instant film was conceived,</i>

00:05:23.055 --> 00:05:24.623
<i> but it took 30 years</i>

00:05:24.656 --> 00:05:26.759
<i> for it to actually come
 to fruition.</i>

00:05:26.792 --> 00:05:32.064 line:0%
The dream of being able to take
a wallet out of my pocket,

00:05:32.097 --> 00:05:35.134 line:0%
and perhaps open the wallet,
press a button,

00:05:35.167 --> 00:05:37.903 line:0%
close the wallet,
and have the picture.

00:05:37.936 --> 00:05:41.374
<i> ( music playing )</i>

00:05:41.407 --> 00:05:43.909
Depew:<i> The SX-70 was really
 the ultimate realization</i>

00:05:43.942 --> 00:05:45.611
<i> of what Edwin Land had in mind</i>

00:05:45.644 --> 00:05:48.948
<i> when he first created
 instant photography.</i>

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:51.617
<i> This camera ushered in
 a whole new film line</i>

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:52.652
called an integral film.

00:05:55.454 --> 00:05:57.523
<i> So this is film
 that's in a sandwich.</i>

00:05:57.556 --> 00:05:59.525
<i> The positive and negative
 and developing chemistry</i>

00:05:59.558 --> 00:06:01.627
<i> live together
 under a Mylar sheet.</i>

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:03.462
There are three chemical pods,

00:06:03.495 --> 00:06:05.998
and these all contain a bunch
of magical chemistry goo.

00:06:06.031 --> 00:06:09.535
Bonanos:<i> And the rollers
 shmush the chemistry</i>

00:06:09.568 --> 00:06:13.672
<i> and they coat the layers
 of film inside the packet.</i>

00:06:13.705 --> 00:06:17.143
<i> Then a number
 of reactions take place.</i>

00:06:17.176 --> 00:06:19.478
 Depew:<i> All these timing layers
 are firing at the same time.</i>

00:06:19.511 --> 00:06:22.181
<i> Everything you would need in
a traditional dark room process</i>

00:06:22.214 --> 00:06:24.884
is happening within
this very thin sheet of film.

00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:27.686
<i> It cannot really be overstated</i>

00:06:27.719 --> 00:06:29.688
<i> how much of a quantum leap
